68.3 133.0 Q 666.7 132.4, 665.6 132.4 Q 664.3 132.4, 663.6 132.9 Q 663.0 133.4, 663.0 134.3 Q 663.0 135.0, 663.3 135.4 Q 663.7 135.8, 664.2 136.1 Q 664.7 136.3, 665.6 136.6 Q 666.7 136.9, 667.4 137.3 Q 668.1 137.6, 668.6 138.4 Q 669.1 139.1, 669.1 140.3 Q 669.1 142.1, 667.9 143.1 Q 666.7 144.0, 664.8 144.0 Q 663.6 144.0, 662.8 143.7 Q 661.9 143.5, 660.9 143.1 L 661.4 141.7 '' fill=''#000000''/></svg>', '["P", "Q", "R", "S"]', 1, 'Optical activity needs a chiral carbon, one with four different groups. In butan-2-ol (Q) the –CH(OH)– carbon carries –H, –OH, –CH₃ and –C₂H₅. Propan-2-ol (P) has two identical –CH₃ groups on that carbon, glycerol (R) has two identical –CH₂OH groups, and 2-methylpropan-2-ol (S) has three –CH₃ groups.', '{"0": "In propan-2-ol the central carbon carries two identical –CH₃ groups.", "2": "In glycerol (propane-1,2,3-triol) the middle carbon carries two identical –CH₂OH groups.", "3": "In 2-methylpropan-2-ol the carbon carrying –OH has three identical –CH₃ groups."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2014', 'Chemistry', 'Fundamental principles of organic chemistry', 'Hybridisation', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'What is the hybridisation of the carbon atoms labelled a, b and c?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 239.6,47.8 L 201.6,113.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 229.9,42.1 L 195.1,101.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 201.6,113.0 L 126.1,112.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 126.1,112.7 L 63.8,112.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 126.0,124.0 L 63.7,123.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 126.1,101.3 L 63.8,101.1'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 203.5,109.7 L 201.6,113.0 L 197.8,113.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 129.9,112.7 L 126.1,112.7 L 123.0,112.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 44.3 98.2 L 53.6 113.2 Q 54.5 114.7, 56.0 117.3 Q 57.5 120.0, 57.5 120.2 L 57.5 98.2 L 61.3 98.2 L 61.3 126.5 L 57.4 126.5 L 47.5 110.1 Q 46.3 108.2, 45.1 106.0 Q 43.9 103.8, 43.5 103.1 L 43.5 126.5 L 39.8 126.5 L 39.8 98.2 L 44.3 98.2 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 251.7 59.7 Q 251.7 58.1, 253.0 57.2 Q 254.4 56.3, 256.8 56.3 L 258.3 56.3 L 258.3 55.9 Q 258.3 54.6, 257.8 54.1 Q 257.2 53.5, 256.0 53.5 Q 255.1 53.5, 254.5 53.7 Q 253.9 53.8, 252.9 54.3 L 252.3 53.0 Q 254.2 52.0, 256.0 52.0 Q 258.2 52.0, 259.2 53.0 Q 260.2 53.9, 260.2 55.9 L 260.2 62.5 L 258.7 62.5 Q 258.7 62.4, 258.6 62.1 Q 258.6 61.8, 258.5 61.3 Q 257.0 62.7, 255.1 62.7 Q 253.6 62.7, 252.6 61.9 Q 251.7 61.1, 251.7 59.7 M 253.6 59.7 Q 253.6 60.4, 254.1 60.9 Q 254.6 61.3, 255.5 61.3 Q 256.3 61.3, 257.0 60.9 Q 257.8 60.5, 258.3 59.9 L 258.3 57.7 L 256.9 57.7 Q 255.3 57.7, 254.4 58.2 Q 253.6 58.7, 253.6 59.7 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 211.1 126.5 Q 213.2 126.5, 214.4 127.9 Q 215.6 129.2, 215.6 131.7 Q 215.6 134.2, 214.2 135.7 Q 212.8 137.1, 210.3 137.1 Q 208.7 137.1, 207.2 136.5 L 206.3 136.3 L 206.3 121.8 L 208.2 121.8 L 208.2 127.8 Q 209.3 126.5, 211.1 126.5 M 210.3 135.7 Q 211.9 135.7, 212.8 134.6 Q 213.7 133.6, 213.7 131.7 Q 213.7 129.9, 213.0 129.0 Q 212.3 128.0, 211.0 128.0 Q 209.9 128.0, 209.2 128.6 Q 208.5 129.1, 208.2 130.1 L 208.2 135.4 Q 209.2 135.7, 210.3 135.7 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 131.3 96.4 Q 131.3 93.9, 132.6 92.5 Q 133.8 91.1, 136.3 91.1 Q 138.6 91.1, 139.8 92.8 L 138.6 93.8 Q 138.1 93.2, 137.5 92.9 Q 137.0 92.6, 136.3 92.6 Q 134.8 92.6, 134.0 93.6 Q 133.3 94.5, 133.3 96.4 Q 133.3 98.3, 134.1 99.3 Q 134.9 100.3, 136.4 100.3 Q 137.2 100.3, 137.8 100.1 Q 138.4 99.9, 139.1 99.6 L 139.6 100.8 Q 138.1 101.7, 136.2 101.7 Q 133.8 101.7, 132.6 100.3 Q 131.3 98.9, 131.3 96.4 '' fill=''#000000''/></svg>', '["a: sp², b: sp², c: sp", "a: sp³, b: sp², c: sp", "a: sp², b: sp, c: sp", "a: sp, b: sp², c: sp²"]', 0, 'Carbons a and b form the C=C double bond. Each has three σ bonds and one π bond, so each is sp² (trigonal planar). Carbon c forms the C≡N triple bond, with two σ bonds and two π bonds, so it is sp (linear).', '{"1": "Carbon a is in a C=C double bond, not a single bond, so it is sp², not sp³.", "2": "Carbon b has a double bond to a and a single bond to c, which gives three σ bonds and sp² hybridisation.", "3": "This reverses the pattern. The triple bond is at carbon c, so c is sp, and the double-bond carbons are sp²."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2015', 'Chemistry', 'Fundamental principles of organic chemistry', 'Sigma and pi bonds', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'How many σ (sigma) and π (pi) bonds are there in one molecule of the compound shown (buta-1,3-diene)?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 264.0,67.8 L 188.0,111.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 257.4,56.3 L 188.0,96.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 188.0,111.6 L 112.0,67.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 112.0,67.6 L 36.0,111.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 112.0,82.8 L 42.6,122.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 191.8,109.4 L 188.0,111.6 L 184.2,109.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 115.8,69.8 L 112.0,67.6 L 108.2,69.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /></svg>', '["9 σ and 2 π", "7 σ and 2 π", "11 σ and 2 π", "9 σ and 4 π"]', 0, 'Buta-1,3-diene is C₄H₆. It has 3 C–C links (two C=C and one C–C) and 6 C–H bonds. Every bond has one σ bond, giving 3 + 6 = 9 σ. Each C=C adds one π bond, giving 2 π.', '{"1": "This counts the C–C links and only some of the C–H bonds. All six C–H bonds are σ bonds.", "2": "There are only six hydrogens (C₄H₆), so there are 6 C–H σ bonds, not 8.", "3": "A double bond has one σ and one π bond, not two π bonds."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2016', 'Chemistry', 'Fundamental principles of organic chemistry', 'Types of carbon atoms', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'The carbon atom labelled x is best described as which type of carbon?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 174.0,49.0 L 207.0,105.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 207.0,105.9 L 239.8,48.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 207.0,105.9 L 264.0,138.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 207.0,105.9 L 150.0,138.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.0,138.9 L 93.0,106.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 93.0,106.0 L 36.0,139.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 93.0,106.0 L 92.9,40.2'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 152.8,137.2 L 150.0,138.9 L 147.1,137.2'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 205.9 122.2 L 202.4 117.3 L 204.6 117.3 L 207.1 120.8 L 209.5 117.3 L 211.5 117.3 L 208.1 122.2 L 211.8 127.5 L 209.6 127.5 L 206.9 123.7 L 204.2 127.5 L 202.1 127.5 L 205.9 122.2 '' fill=''#000000''/></svg>', '["Tertiary (3°)", "Secondary (2°)", "Quaternary (4°)", "Primary (1°)"]', 2, 'Carbon x is bonded to four other carbon atoms (three methyl groups and the –CH₂– of the chain) and to no hydrogen. A carbon bonded to four carbons is quaternary.', '{"0": "A tertiary carbon is bonded to three carbons and one hydrogen, like the –CH– further along this chain.", "1": "A secondary carbon is bonded to two carbons, like the –CH₂– in this chain.", "3": "A primary carbon is bonded to only one carbon, like the –CH₃ groups."}', 1)
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, figure, options, answer, why, wrong, mdcat, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11, s.column12, s.column13,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-2017', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Naming alkanes', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'What is the IUPAC name of the alkane shown?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 36.0,138.8 L 93.0,106.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 93.0,106.0 L 93.1,40.2'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 93.0,106.0 L 150.0,138.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.0,138.9 L 207.0,106.1'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 207.0,106.1 L 264.0,139.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 207.0,106.1 L 207.1,40.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 147.2,137.3 L 150.0,138.9 L 152.9,137.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /></svg>', '["2,4-dimethylhexane", "2-methyl-4-methylpentane", "2,4-dimethylpentane", "Heptane"]', 2, 'The longest chain has five carbons (pentane). Methyl groups sit on carbons 2 and 4 from either end. Identical groups are combined with a prefix, giving 2,4-dimethylpentane (C₇H₁₆).', '{"0": "Counting a methyl branch into the main chain gives six carbons, but the longest continuous chain has only five.", "1": "Repeated substituents are combined using di-, tri- and so on, not listed separately.", "3": "Heptane is the straight-chain C₇H₁₆. This molecule is branched."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2018', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Naming alkanes', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'What is the IUPAC name of the compound shown?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 144.0,29.7 L 188.0,105.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 188.0,105.6 L 231.8,29.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 188.0,105.6 L 264.0,149.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 188.0,105.6 L 112.0,149.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 112.0,149.6 L 36.0,105.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 115.8,147.4 L 112.0,149.6 L 108.2,147.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /></svg>', '["3,3-dimethylbutane", "2,2-dimethylbutane", "2-ethyl-2-methylpropane", "Hexane"]', 1, 'The longest chain has four carbons (butane). Numbering from the end nearer the branches puts both methyl groups on carbon 2, giving 2,2-dimethylbutane.', '{"0": "Numbering must start from the end that gives the lowest locants: 2,2 rather than 3,3.", "2": "The ethyl group is part of the longest chain, which has four carbons, not three.", "3": "Hexane is unbranched. This molecule has a carbon carrying two methyl groups."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2019', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Naming alkanes', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'What is the IUPAC name of the alkane shown?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 106.0,157.6 L 105.9,123.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 105.9,123.6 L 135.3,106.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 135.3,106.6 L 164.7,123.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 164.7,123.5 L 194.1,106.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 135.3,106.6 L 135.2,72.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 135.2,72.6 L 164.6,55.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 164.6,55.6 L 164.5,21.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 164.6,55.6 L 194.0,72.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 105.9,125.3 L 105.9,123.6 L 107.4,122.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 163.3,122.7 L 164.7,123.5 L 166.2,122.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 135.2,74.3 L 135.2,72.6 L 136.7,71.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /></svg>', '["4-ethyl-2-methylhexane", "3-ethyl-5-methylhexane", "2-methyl-4-ethylhexane", "2,4-diethylpentane"]', 0, 'The longest chain has six carbons (hexane). Numbering from the end nearer the first branch gives locants 2 (methyl) and 4 (ethyl), which are lower than 3 and 5. Substituents are listed in alphabetical order, so ethyl comes before methyl: 4-ethyl-2-methylhexane.', '{"1": "Numbering from the other end gives 3 and 5. The lowest set of locants, 2 and 4, is correct.", "2": "The locants are right but the prefixes must be in alphabetical order, so ethyl comes before methyl.", "3": "A five-carbon chain is not the longest. A chain through one ethyl group has six carbons."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2020', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Naming alkenes', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'What is the IUPAC name of the alkene shown?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 197.0,157.6 L 197.1,103.2'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 197.1,103.2 L 150.1,76.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 188.9,107.9 L 150.0,85.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.1,76.0 L 150.2,21.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.1,76.0 L 102.9,103.1'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 197.1,106.0 L 197.1,103.2 L 194.7,101.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /></svg>', '["3-methylbut-2-ene", "2-methylbut-3-ene", "Pent-2-ene", "2-methylbut-2-ene"]', 3, 'The longest chain containing the C=C has four carbons (butene). Numbering from the end that gives the double bond the lowest number puts it at C2 (but-2-ene). From either end the double bond is at 2, so the methyl group then takes the lowest number, also 2: 2-methylbut-2-ene.', '{"0": "When the double bond gets 2 from both ends, the substituent gets the lower number, which is 2, not 3.", "1": "The double bond must get the lowest possible number, which is 2, not 3.", "2": "The chain has four carbons with a methyl branch, not five carbons in a row."}', 1)
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, figure, options, answer, why, wrong, mdcat, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11, s.column12, s.column13,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-2021', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Naming alkenes', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'What is the IUPAC name of the alkene shown?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 197.0,21.6 L 197.1,76.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 188.8,21.6 L 188.9,71.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 197.1,76.0 L 150.1,103.2'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.1,103.2 L 150.2,157.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.1,103.2 L 102.9,76.1'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 197.1,73.2 L 197.1,76.0 L 194.7,77.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /></svg>', '["2-methylbut-3-ene", "3-methylbut-2-ene", "3-methylbut-1-ene", "2-methylbut-1-ene"]', 2, 'Number from the end nearer the C=C so that the double bond is at C1 (but-1-ene). The methyl group is then on C3: 3-methylbut-1-ene.', '{"0": "The double bond gets the lowest number, so numbering starts at the =CH₂ end and gives but-1-ene, not but-3-ene.", "1": "The double bond is at the end of the chain (C1–C2), not in the middle.", "3": "In 2-methylbut-1-ene the methyl group would sit on a carbon of the double bond. Here it is on the next carbon."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2022', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Naming alkynes', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'What is the IUPAC name of the alkyne shown?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 36.0,90.6 L 101.1,90.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 101.1,90.3 L 166.1,90.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 101.0,80.6 L 166.1,80.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 101.1,100.1 L 166.2,99.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 166.1,90.0 L 231.2,89.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 231.2,89.7 L 264.0,146.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 231.2,89.7 L 263.5,33.2'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 97.8,90.3 L 101.1,90.3 L 104.3,90.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 162.9,90.0 L 166.1,90.0 L 169.4,90.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /></svg>', '["2-methylpent-3-yne", "4-methylpent-2-yne", "4-methylpent-3-yne", "4-methylhex-2-yne"]', 1, 'The longest chain containing the C≡C has five carbons. Numbering from the end nearer the triple bond puts it at C2 (pent-2-yne) and the methyl group at C4: 4-methylpent-2-yne.', '{"0": "Numbering must give the triple bond the lowest number (2), even though this gives the methyl group a higher number.", "2": "The triple bond starts at carbon 2 when counted from the nearer end, not at 3.", "3": "The main chain has five carbons, not six."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2023', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Acidity of alkynes', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Which of the compounds shown gives a white precipitate with ammoniacal silver nitrate solution?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''760px'' height=''160px'' viewBox=''0 0 760 160''><path d=''M 36.6,67.5 L 95.0,67.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 95.0,67.5 L 153.4,67.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 95.0,58.7 L 153.4,58.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 95.0,76.3 L 153.4,76.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 92.1,67.5 L 95.0,67.5 L 97.9,67.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 94.7 131.3 Q 96.9 131.3, 98.0 132.2 Q 99.1 133.2, 99.1 135.0 Q 99.1 136.8, 97.9 137.8 Q 96.8 138.8, 94.7 138.8 L 92.7 138.8 L 92.7 144.0 L 90.9 144.0 L 90.9 131.3 L 94.7 131.3 M 94.7 137.3 Q 95.9 137.3, 96.6 136.7 Q 97.3 136.1, 97.3 135.0 Q 97.3 133.9, 96.6 133.3 Q 96.0 132.7, 94.7 132.7 L 92.7 132.7 L 92.7 137.3 L 94.7 137.3 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 209.0,111.0 L 259.7,82.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 259.7,82.0 L 310.3,53.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 255.3,74.4 L 306.0,45.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 264.0,89.6 L 314.7,60.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 310.3,53.0 L 361.0,24.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 257.1,83.5 L 259.7,82.0 L 262.2,80.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 307.8,54.4 L 310.3,53.0 L 312.9,51.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 287.9 142.4 Q 288.6 142.4, 289.2 142.2 L 289.8 143.7 Q 288.7 144.0, 287.8 144.0 Q 286.2 144.0, 285.2 143.0 Q 284.2 142.1, 284.0 140.3 Q 281.6 140.0, 280.4 138.3 Q 279.1 136.5, 279.1 133.7 Q 279.1 130.7, 280.7 129.0 Q 282.2 127.3, 285.0 127.3 Q 287.8 127.3, 289.3 129.0 Q 290.9 130.7, 290.9 133.7 Q 290.9 136.7, 289.4 138.5 Q 288.0 140.2, 285.4 140.3 Q 285.6 141.3, 286.3 141.9 Q 287.0 142.4, 287.9 142.4 M 280.9 133.7 Q 280.9 136.3, 282.0 137.6 Q 283.1 138.9, 285.0 138.9 Q 286.9 138.9, 288.0 137.6 Q 289.1 136.3, 289.1 133.7 Q 289.1 131.2, 288.0 130.0 Q 286.9 128.7, 285.0 128.7 Q 283.1 128.7, 282.0 130.0 Q 280.9 131.2, 280.9 133.7 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 424.4,57.0 L 475.1,86.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 475.1,86.0 L 525.6,56.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 475.1,75.9 L 521.2,49.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 472.6,84.5 L 475.1,86.0 L 477.6,84.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 477.5 144.0 L 474.4 138.8 L 474.4 138.8 L 472.3 138.8 L 472.3 144.0 L 470.6 144.0 L 470.6 131.3 L 474.4 131.3 Q 476.6 131.3, 477.7 132.2 Q 478.8 133.2, 478.8 135.0 Q 478.8 136.3, 478.1 137.3 Q 477.4 138.2, 476.2 138.5 L 479.4 144.0 L 477.5 144.0 M 472.3 137.3 L 474.4 137.3 Q 475.6 137.3, 476.3 136.7 Q 477.0 136.1, 477.0 135.0 Q 477.0 133.9, 476.3 133.3 Q 475.6 132.7, 474.4 132.7 L 472.3 132.7 L 472.3 137.3 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 614.4,81.8 L 665.1,52.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 665.1,52.8 L 715.6,82.2'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 662.6,54.3 L 665.1,52.8 L 667.6,54.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 661.4 141.7 Q 661.5 141.7, 662.1 142.0 Q 662.7 142.2, 663.4 142.4 Q 664.0 142.5, 664.7 142.5 Q 665.9 142.5, 666.6 142.0 Q 667.3 141.4, 667.3 140.3 Q 667.3 139.6, 666.9 139.2 Q 666.6 138.8, 666.1 138.5 Q 665.5 138.3, 664.6 138.0 Q 663.5 137.7, 662.8 137.4 Q 662.1 137.1, 661.7 136.4 Q 661.2 135.7, 661.2 134.5 Q 661.2 132.9, 662.3 131.9 Q 663.4 131.0, 665.5 131.0 Q 667.0 131.0, 668.7 131.7 L 668.3 133.0 Q 666.7 132.4, 665.6 132.4 Q 664.3 132.4, 663.6 132.9 Q 663.0 133.4, 663.0 134.3 Q 663.0 135.0, 663.3 135.4 Q 663.7 135.8, 664.2 136.1 Q 664.7 136.3, 665.6 136.6 Q 666.7 136.9, 667.4 137.3 Q 668.1 137.6, 668.6 138.4 Q 669.1 139.1, 669.1 140.3 Q 669.1 142.1, 667.9 143.1 Q 666.7 144.0, 664.8 144.0 Q 663.6 144.0, 662.8 143.7 Q 661.9 143.5, 660.9 143.1 L 661.4 141.7 '' fill=''#000000''/></svg>', '["P", "Q", "R", "S"]', 0, 'A hydrogen on a triple-bonded carbon (≡C–H) is weakly acidic because the sp carbon holds electrons tightly. Propyne (P) is a terminal alkyne, so it replaces this H with silver and forms a white precipitate of silver propynide. But-2-yne (Q) has no ≡C–H, and alkenes and alkanes are not acidic enough.', '{"1": "But-2-yne''s triple bond is in the middle of the chain, so there is no hydrogen on an sp carbon.", "2": "Propene''s =C–H hydrogens are on sp² carbons, which are far less acidic.", "3": "Propane has only sp³ C–H bonds, which do not react."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2024', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Reactions of alkenes', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'The alkene shown reacts with hydrogen bromide (no peroxide present). What is the major product?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 36.0,65.9 L 150.2,131.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.2,131.3 L 264.0,65.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.2,108.5 L 254.1,47.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 144.5,128.0 L 150.2,131.3 L 155.9,127.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /></svg>', '["1-bromopropane", "2-bromopropane", "1,2-dibromopropane", "Propane"]', 1, 'By Markovnikov''s rule the hydrogen of HBr adds to the carbon of the C=C that already has more hydrogens (=CH₂), and Br adds to the middle carbon. The more stable secondary carbocation forms, so the major product is 2-bromopropane.', '{"0": "1-bromopropane is the anti-Markovnikov product, which needs a peroxide (free-radical) route.", "2": "HBr adds one H and one Br. A dibromide forms when Br₂ adds.", "3": "Propane forms when H₂ adds (hydrogenation), not HBr."}', 1)
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, figure, options, answer, why, wrong, mdcat, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11, s.column12, s.column13,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-2025', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Reactions of alkynes', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'The alkyne shown is warmed with dilute sulphuric acid and mercury(II) sulphate. What is the product?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 36.0,89.6 L 150.0,89.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.0,89.6 L 264.0,89.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.0,72.5 L 264.0,72.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.0,106.7 L 264.0,106.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 144.3,89.6 L 150.0,89.6 L 155.7,89.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /></svg>', '["Propanone", "Propanal", "Propan-1-ol", "Propan-2-ol"]', 0, 'Water adds across the triple bond following Markovnikov''s rule, with –OH going to the middle carbon. The enol formed, CH₃C(OH)=CH₂, rearranges at once to the ketone, CH₃COCH₃ (propanone).', '{"1": "An aldehyde forms only from ethyne. In propyne the –OH adds to the inner carbon, which gives a ketone.", "2": "The intermediate is an enol, which turns into a carbonyl compound, not a saturated alcohol.", "3": "Only one water molecule adds. The enol rearranges to a ketone and is not reduced to an alcohol."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2026', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Preparation of alkenes', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'The alcohol shown is heated with excess concentrated sulphuric acid. What is the main organic product?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 248.8,153.6 L 182.9,115.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 182.9,115.7 L 182.8,56.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 182.9,115.7 L 117.1,153.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 117.1,153.9 L 51.2,116.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 120.4,152.0 L 117.1,153.9 L 113.8,152.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 169.7 39.7 Q 169.7 32.9, 173.1 29.1 Q 176.5 25.3, 182.7 25.3 Q 189.0 25.3, 192.4 29.1 Q 195.7 32.9, 195.7 39.7 Q 195.7 46.6, 192.3 50.5 Q 188.9 54.4, 182.7 54.4 Q 176.5 54.4, 173.1 50.5 Q 169.7 46.7, 169.7 39.7 M 182.7 51.2 Q 187.1 51.2, 189.4 48.3 Q 191.7 45.4, 191.7 39.7 Q 191.7 34.2, 189.4 31.4 Q 187.1 28.5, 182.7 28.5 Q 178.4 28.5, 176.1 31.3 Q 173.7 34.1, 173.7 39.7 Q 173.7 45.5, 176.1 48.3 Q 178.4 51.2, 182.7 51.2 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 200.2 25.7 L 204.0 25.7 L 204.0 37.7 L 218.5 37.7 L 218.5 25.7 L 222.3 25.7 L 222.3 54.0 L 218.5 54.0 L 218.5 40.9 L 204.0 40.9 L 204.0 54.0 L 200.2 54.0 L 200.2 25.7 '' fill=''#000000''/></svg>', '["But-1-ene", "But-2-ene", "Butane", "2-methylpropene"]', 1, 'Concentrated H₂SO₄ removes water (dehydration). Water can be lost towards C1 or C3. Removing H from C3 gives the more substituted, more stable alkene, but-2-ene, which is the main product (Saytzeff''s rule).', '{"0": "But-1-ene forms too, but less of it, because its C=C is less substituted.", "2": "Dehydration makes an alkene, not an alkane. Nothing is reduced.", "3": "The carbon chain stays unbranched. 2-methylpropene would need a branched skeleton."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2027', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Free radical substitution', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'The alkane shown reacts with chlorine in sunlight. How many different monochloro products (C₄H₉Cl) can form?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 150.1,21.6 L 150.0,112.2'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.0,112.2 L 228.5,157.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.0,112.2 L 71.5,157.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /></svg>', '["1", "3", "4", "2"]', 3, '2-methylpropane has two kinds of hydrogen: nine equivalent H on the three –CH₃ groups and one H on the central –CH–. Replacing one of each kind gives 1-chloro-2-methylpropane and 2-chloro-2-methylpropane, so there are two products.', '{"0": "There are two kinds of hydrogen, so there are two different products.", "1": "The three –CH₃ groups are equivalent, so they give only one product between them.", "2": "There are only two kinds of hydrogen, giving two products, not four."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2028', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Free radical substitution', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'How many different monochloro isomers can form when the alkane shown reacts with chlorine in light?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 264.0,155.3 L 188.0,111.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 188.0,111.5 L 187.9,23.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 188.0,111.5 L 112.0,155.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 112.0,155.4 L 36.0,111.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 115.8,153.2 L 112.0,155.4 L 108.2,153.2'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /></svg>', '["3", "2", "4", "5"]', 2, '2-methylbutane has four kinds of hydrogen: the two equivalent –CH₃ groups on C2, the single H on C2, the –CH₂– at C3 and the –CH₃ at C4. Each kind gives a different product, so four monochloro isomers form.', '{"0": "The –CH₃ on C4 is different from the two –CH₃ groups on C2, so it gives a separate product.", "1": "There are more kinds of hydrogen than this. Every different carbon carrying H gives its own product.", "3": "The two methyl groups on C2 are equivalent, so they give only one product between them."}', 1)
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, figure, options, answer, why, wrong, mdcat, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11, s.column12, s.column13,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-2029', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Electrophilic substitution in benzene', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'The compound shown is nitrated with a mixture of concentrated nitric and sulphuric acids. Where does the –NO₂ group mainly go?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 61.6,157.5 L 120.5,123.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 120.5,123.5 L 120.6,55.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 130.7,117.7 L 130.8,61.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 120.6,55.5 L 179.5,21.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 179.5,21.6 L 238.4,55.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 179.5,33.4 L 228.2,61.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 238.4,55.6 L 238.3,123.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 238.3,123.6 L 179.4,157.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 228.1,117.7 L 179.4,145.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 179.4,157.6 L 120.5,123.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 120.6,58.9 L 120.6,55.5 L 123.5,53.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 176.6,23.3 L 179.5,21.6 L 182.5,23.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 235.4,53.9 L 238.4,55.6 L 238.4,59.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 238.3,120.2 L 238.3,123.6 L 235.4,125.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 182.3,155.9 L 179.4,157.6 L 176.5,155.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /></svg>', '["Meta to the methyl group", "Ortho and para to the methyl group", "On the methyl group", "Equally at all ring positions"]', 1, 'The methyl group pushes electrons into the ring and activates it. It directs incoming electrophiles to the ortho (2) and para (4) positions, so the main products are 2-nitro- and 4-nitromethylbenzene.', '{"0": "Meta direction is shown by electron-withdrawing groups such as –NO₂, –CHO or –COOH, not by –CH₃.", "2": "Nitration with mixed acid is substitution on the ring, not on the side chain.", "3": "A substituent always makes some ring positions more reactive than others, so the products are not spread equally."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2030', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Electrophilic substitution in benzene', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'The compound shown reacts with bromine in the presence of FeBr₃. What is the main product?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 125.8,35.7 L 137.6,42.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 123.2,40.4 L 134.9,47.1'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 164.9,38.4 L 165.6,38.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 145.1,61.3 L 145.2,85.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 145.2,85.8 L 176.3,103.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 145.2,92.0 L 170.9,106.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 176.3,103.7 L 176.4,139.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 176.4,139.6 L 145.3,157.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 171.0,136.5 L 145.3,151.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 145.3,157.6 L 114.2,139.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 114.2,139.7 L 114.1,103.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 119.6,136.6 L 119.5,106.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 114.1,103.8 L 145.2,85.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 174.8,102.8 L 176.3,103.7 L 176.3,105.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 176.4,137.8 L 176.4,139.6 L 174.8,140.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 146.9,156.7 L 145.3,157.6 L 143.8,156.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 115.8,140.6 L 114.2,139.7 L 114.2,137.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 114.1,105.6 L 114.1,103.8 L 115.7,102.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 104.6 32.1 Q 104.6 27.2, 107.1 24.5 Q 109.5 21.7, 114.0 21.7 Q 118.5 21.7, 120.9 24.5 Q 123.3 27.2, 123.3 32.1 Q 123.3 37.0, 120.9 39.8 Q 118.4 42.6, 114.0 42.6 Q 109.5 42.6, 107.1 39.8 Q 104.6 37.0, 104.6 32.1 M 114.0 40.3 Q 117.1 40.3, 118.7 38.2 Q 120.4 36.1, 120.4 32.1 Q 120.4 28.1, 118.7 26.1 Q 117.1 24.0, 114.0 24.0 Q 110.9 24.0, 109.2 26.0 Q 107.5 28.0, 107.5 32.1 Q 107.5 36.2, 109.2 38.2 Q 110.9 40.3, 114.0 40.3 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 140.6 39.7 L 147.3 50.5 Q 147.9 51.6, 149.0 53.5 Q 150.1 55.4, 150.1 55.5 L 150.1 39.7 L 152.8 39.7 L 152.8 60.1 L 150.0 60.1 L 142.9 48.3 Q 142.1 46.9, 141.2 45.3 Q 140.3 43.8, 140.0 43.3 L 140.0 60.1 L 137.4 60.1 L 137.4 39.7 L 140.6 39.7 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 156.6 43.4 L 160.2 43.4 L 160.2 39.6 L 161.8 39.6 L 161.8 43.4 L 165.5 43.4 L 165.5 44.8 L 161.8 44.8 L 161.8 48.6 L 160.2 48.6 L 160.2 44.8 L 156.6 44.8 L 156.6 43.4 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 166.8 31.9 Q 166.8 27.1, 169.2 24.3 Q 171.7 21.6, 176.2 21.6 Q 180.7 21.6, 183.1 24.3 Q 185.5 27.1, 185.5 31.9 Q 185.5 36.9, 183.1 39.7 Q 180.6 42.5, 176.2 42.5 Q 171.7 42.5, 169.2 39.7 Q 166.8 36.9, 166.8 31.9 M 176.2 40.2 Q 179.3 40.2, 180.9 38.1 Q 182.6 36.0, 182.6 31.9 Q 182.6 27.9, 180.9 25.9 Q 179.3 23.9, 176.2 23.9 Q 173.1 23.9, 171.4 25.9 Q 169.7 27.9, 169.7 31.9 Q 169.7 36.0, 171.4 38.1 Q 173.1 40.2, 176.2 40.2 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 188.4 24.9 L 195.4 24.9 L 195.4 26.4 L 188.4 26.4 L 188.4 24.9 '' fill=''#000000''/></svg>', '["1-bromo-3-nitrobenzene", "1-bromo-4-nitrobenzene", "1-bromo-2-nitrobenzene", "2,4,6-tribromonitrobenzene"]', 0, 'The –NO₂ group strongly withdraws electrons from the ring, which deactivates it and directs the incoming bromine to the meta (3) position. The main product is 1-bromo-3-nitrobenzene.', '{"1": "Para substitution is favoured by electron-donating groups. –NO₂ is electron-withdrawing.", "2": "Ortho substitution is also favoured by electron-donating groups, not by –NO₂.", "3": "The –NO₂ group deactivates the ring, so substituting three times is very hard. Phenol, not nitrobenzene, gives a tribromo product easily."}', 1),
