FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-2033', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Naming benzene derivatives', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'What is the IUPAC name of the compound shown?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 36.0,155.3 L 93.0,122.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 93.0,122.5 L 93.1,56.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 93.1,56.7 L 150.1,23.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 102.9,62.4 L 150.0,35.2'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.1,23.8 L 207.0,56.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 207.0,56.7 L 264.0,23.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 207.0,56.7 L 206.9,122.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 197.1,62.4 L 197.1,116.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 206.9,122.5 L 149.9,155.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 149.9,155.4 L 93.0,122.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.0,144.0 L 102.9,116.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 93.0,60.0 L 93.1,56.7 L 95.9,55.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 147.2,25.5 L 150.1,23.8 L 152.9,25.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 206.9,119.2 L 206.9,122.5 L 204.1,124.2'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 152.8,153.7 L 149.9,155.4 L 147.1,153.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /></svg>', '["1,3-dimethylbenzene", "1,2-dimethylbenzene", "1,4-dimethylbenzene", "Ethylbenzene"]', 2, 'Two methyl groups sit opposite each other on the ring, at positions 1 and 4 (the para positions). The name is 1,4-dimethylbenzene (para-xylene).', '{"0": "1,3 (meta) means the groups are one ring carbon apart. Here they are directly opposite.", "1": "1,2 (ortho) means the groups are on neighbouring carbons.", "3": "Ethylbenzene has one –CH₂CH₃ group, not two separate methyl groups."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2034', 'Chemistry', 'Organic chemistry', 'Addition reactions of benzene', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'The compound shown is heated with excess hydrogen over a nickel catalyst. What is the product?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 61.6,157.5 L 120.5,123.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 120.5,123.5 L 120.6,55.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 130.7,117.7 L 130.8,61.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 120.6,55.5 L 179.5,21.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 179.5,21.6 L 238.4,55.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 179.5,33.4 L 228.2,61.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 238.4,55.6 L 238.3,123.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 238.3,123.6 L 179.4,157.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 228.1,117.7 L 179.4,145.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 179.4,157.6 L 120.5,123.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 120.6,58.9 L 120.6,55.5 L 123.5,53.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 176.6,23.3 L 179.5,21.6 L 182.5,23.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 235.4,53.9 L 238.4,55.6 L 238.4,59.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 238.3,120.2 L 238.3,123.6 L 235.4,125.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 182.3,155.9 L 179.4,157.6 L 176.5,155.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /></svg>', '["Methylcyclohexane", "Cyclohexane and methane", "1-methylcyclohexene", "Phenylmethanol"]', 0, 'Under these forcing conditions three molecules of H₂ add across the ring, and the aromatic ring becomes a saturated cyclohexane ring. The methyl group is unchanged: C₆H₅CH₃ + 3H₂ → C₆H₁₁CH₃ (methylcyclohexane).', '{"1": "Hydrogenation adds hydrogen to the ring and does not break the C–CH₃ bond.", "2": "With excess hydrogen the ring is fully saturated. A cyclohexene would still have a C=C.", "3": "Phenylmethanol needs oxygen added, not hydrogen."}', 1),
