L 615.1 69.0 L 617.3 69.0 L 617.3 85.0 L 615.1 85.0 L 615.1 77.6 L 607.0 77.6 L 607.0 85.0 L 604.8 85.0 L 604.8 69.0 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 620.5 77.0 Q 620.5 73.1, 622.4 71.0 Q 624.3 68.9, 627.8 68.9 Q 631.4 68.9, 633.3 71.0 Q 635.1 73.1, 635.1 77.0 Q 635.1 80.8, 633.2 83.0 Q 631.3 85.2, 627.8 85.2 Q 624.3 85.2, 622.4 83.0 Q 620.5 80.8, 620.5 77.0 M 627.8 83.4 Q 630.3 83.4, 631.6 81.8 Q 632.9 80.1, 632.9 77.0 Q 632.9 73.8, 631.6 72.3 Q 630.3 70.7, 627.8 70.7 Q 625.4 70.7, 624.1 72.2 Q 622.8 73.8, 622.8 77.0 Q 622.8 80.2, 624.1 81.8 Q 625.4 83.4, 627.8 83.4 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 661.4 141.7 Q 661.5 141.7, 662.1 142.0 Q 662.7 142.2, 663.4 142.4 Q 664.0 142.5, 664.7 142.5 Q 665.9 142.5, 666.6 142.0 Q 667.3 141.4, 667.3 140.3 Q 667.3 139.6, 666.9 139.2 Q 666.6 138.8, 666.1 138.5 Q 665.5 138.3, 664.6 138.0 Q 663.5 137.7, 662.8 137.4 Q 662.1 137.1, 661.7 136.4 Q 661.2 135.7, 661.2 134.5 Q 661.2 132.9, 662.3 131.9 Q 663.4 131.0, 665.5 131.0 Q 667.0 131.0, 668.7 131.7 L 668.3 133.0 Q 666.7 132.4, 665.6 132.4 Q 664.3 132.4, 663.6 132.9 Q 663.0 133.4, 663.0 134.3 Q 663.0 135.0, 663.3 135.4 Q 663.7 135.8, 664.2 136.1 Q 664.7 136.3, 665.6 136.6 Q 666.7 136.9, 667.4 137.3 Q 668.1 137.6, 668.6 138.4 Q 669.1 139.1, 669.1 140.3 Q 669.1 142.1, 667.9 143.1 Q 666.7 144.0, 664.8 144.0 Q 663.6 144.0, 662.8 143.7 Q 661.9 143.5, 660.9 143.1 L 661.4 141.7 '' fill=''#000000''/></svg>', '["P", "Q", "S", "R"]', 3, 'Lucas reagent changes an alcohol into an insoluble chloroalkane, which makes the solution cloudy. Tertiary alcohols react at once because they form a stable tertiary carbocation. 2-methylpropan-2-ol (R) is tertiary. Secondary alcohols take about five minutes, and primary alcohols show no change at room temperature.', '{"0": "Butan-1-ol is primary and shows no cloudiness at room temperature.", "1": "Butan-2-ol is secondary and turns cloudy only after a few minutes.", "2": "2-methylpropan-1-ol is primary (its –OH carbon is –CH₂–), so it does not react at room temperature."}', 1)
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, figure, options, answer, why, wrong, mdcat, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11, s.column12, s.column13,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('CHE-2045', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols and phenols', 'Oxidation of alcohols', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'The alcohol shown is warmed with acidified potassium dichromate(VI). What is the organic product?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 215.8,153.9 L 150.1,115.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.1,115.6 L 84.2,153.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 150.1,115.6 L 150.3,56.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 137.4 39.7 Q 137.4 32.9, 140.8 29.1 Q 144.1 25.3, 150.4 25.3 Q 156.7 25.3, 160.1 29.1 Q 163.4 32.9, 163.4 39.7 Q 163.4 46.6, 160.0 50.5 Q 156.6 54.4, 150.4 54.4 Q 144.2 54.4, 140.8 50.5 Q 137.4 46.6, 137.4 39.7 M 150.4 51.2 Q 154.7 51.2, 157.1 48.3 Q 159.4 45.4, 159.4 39.7 Q 159.4 34.2, 157.1 31.4 Q 154.7 28.5, 150.4 28.5 Q 146.1 28.5, 143.7 31.3 Q 141.4 34.1, 141.4 39.7 Q 141.4 45.4, 143.7 48.3 Q 146.1 51.2, 150.4 51.2 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 167.8 25.6 L 171.7 25.6 L 171.7 37.7 L 186.1 37.7 L 186.1 25.6 L 190.0 25.6 L 190.0 54.0 L 186.1 54.0 L 186.1 40.9 L 171.7 40.9 L 171.7 54.0 L 167.8 54.0 L 167.8 25.6 '' fill=''#000000''/></svg>', '["Propanal", "Propanone", "Propanoic acid", "Propene"]', 1, 'Propan-2-ol is a secondary alcohol. Oxidation removes the H from the –OH and the H from the carbon carrying it, giving a C=O between two carbons: the ketone propanone. The orange dichromate turns green.', '{"0": "Aldehydes come from primary alcohols. A secondary alcohol gives a ketone.", "2": "A carboxylic acid needs a primary alcohol oxidised all the way.", "3": "Propene would come from dehydration, not from oxidation with dichromate."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2046', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols and phenols', 'Oxidation of alcohols', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'The alcohol shown is warmed with acidified potassium dichromate(VI). What is observed?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 104.3,40.3 L 145.2,111.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 145.2,111.7 L 216.3,153.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 145.2,111.7 L 73.9,152.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 145.2,111.7 L 176.4,58.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 173.6 40.6 Q 173.6 33.8, 176.9 30.0 Q 180.3 26.2, 186.6 26.2 Q 192.8 26.2, 196.2 30.0 Q 199.6 33.8, 199.6 40.6 Q 199.6 47.5, 196.2 51.4 Q 192.8 55.3, 186.6 55.3 Q 180.3 55.3, 176.9 51.4 Q 173.6 47.5, 173.6 40.6 M 186.6 52.1 Q 190.9 52.1, 193.2 49.2 Q 195.6 46.3, 195.6 40.6 Q 195.6 35.1, 193.2 32.3 Q 190.9 29.4, 186.6 29.4 Q 182.2 29.4, 179.9 32.2 Q 177.6 35.0, 177.6 40.6 Q 177.6 46.3, 179.9 49.2 Q 182.2 52.1, 186.6 52.1 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 204.0 26.5 L 207.8 26.5 L 207.8 38.6 L 222.3 38.6 L 222.3 26.5 L 226.1 26.5 L 226.1 54.9 L 222.3 54.9 L 222.3 41.8 L 207.8 41.8 L 207.8 54.9 L 204.0 54.9 L 204.0 26.5 '' fill=''#000000''/></svg>', '["The solution stays orange", "The solution turns green and a ketone forms", "The solution turns green and an aldehyde forms", "A silver mirror forms"]', 0, '2-methylpropan-2-ol is a tertiary alcohol. The carbon carrying –OH has no hydrogen to lose, so it is not oxidised under these conditions and the dichromate stays orange.', '{"1": "A ketone forms from a secondary alcohol, whose –OH carbon carries one hydrogen.", "2": "An aldehyde forms from a primary alcohol, whose –OH carbon carries two hydrogens.", "3": "A silver mirror is the Tollens'' test for aldehydes. It does not involve dichromate."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2047', 'Chemistry', 'Alcohols and phenols', 'Reactions of phenol', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Bromine water is added to an aqueous solution of the compound shown. What happens?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 108.4,132.8 L 144.8,111.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 144.8,111.9 L 144.8,52.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 153.7,106.7 L 153.7,57.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 144.8,52.6 L 196.2,23.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 196.2,23.0 L 247.5,52.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 196.2,33.2 L 238.6,57.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 247.5,52.7 L 247.5,111.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 247.5,111.9 L 196.1,141.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 238.6,106.8 L 196.1,131.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 196.1,141.6 L 144.8,111.9'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 144.8,55.5 L 144.8,52.6 L 147.4,51.1'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 193.6,24.5 L 196.2,23.0 L 198.8,24.5'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 245.0,51.2 L 247.5,52.7 L 247.5,55.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 247.5,109.0 L 247.5,111.9 L 244.9,113.4'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 198.7,140.1 L 196.1,141.6 L 193.6,140.1'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 52.5 127.5 L 56.3 127.5 L 56.3 139.5 L 70.8 139.5 L 70.8 127.5 L 74.6 127.5 L 74.6 155.8 L 70.8 155.8 L 70.8 142.7 L 56.3 142.7 L 56.3 155.8 L 52.5 155.8 L 52.5 127.5 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 80.4 141.5 Q 80.4 134.7, 83.8 130.9 Q 87.2 127.1, 93.4 127.1 Q 99.7 127.1, 103.1 130.9 Q 106.4 134.7, 106.4 141.5 Q 106.4 148.4, 103.0 152.3 Q 99.6 156.2, 93.4 156.2 Q 87.2 156.2, 83.8 152.3 Q 80.4 148.5, 80.4 141.5 M 93.4 153.0 Q 97.8 153.0, 100.1 150.1 Q 102.4 147.2, 102.4 141.5 Q 102.4 136.0, 100.1 133.2 Q 97.8 130.3, 93.4 130.3 Q 89.1 130.3, 86.8 133.1 Q 84.4 135.9, 84.4 141.5 Q 84.4 147.3, 86.8 150.1 Q 89.1 153.0, 93.4 153.0 '' fill=''#000000''/></svg>', '["Nothing happens without an FeBr₃ catalyst", "A white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol forms at once", "Only bromobenzene forms, slowly", "The –OH group is replaced by –Br"]', 1, 'The –OH group strongly activates the ring at the 2, 4 and 6 positions. Phenol decolourises bromine water at once at room temperature, with no catalyst, and gives a white precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol.', '{"0": "Benzene needs a catalyst, but phenol''s ring is so activated that it reacts without one.", "2": "Bromine substitutes hydrogens on the ring. The –OH stays, so bromobenzene does not form.", "3": "The C–O bond in phenol is strong (partly double-bond character) and is not replaced by bromine."}', 1),
