  ('CHE-2055', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Reduction of carbonyl compounds', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'The compound shown is reduced with sodium borohydride (NaBH₄). What is the product?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 203.5,21.6 L 203.6,76.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 203.6,76.0 L 156.6,103.2'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 156.6,103.2 L 156.7,157.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 160.6,100.9 L 126.2,81.1'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 156.6,107.9 L 122.2,88.2'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 203.6,73.2 L 203.6,76.0 L 201.2,77.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 158.9,101.9 L 156.6,103.2 L 156.6,106.0'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 96.4 76.2 Q 96.4 69.4, 99.8 65.6 Q 103.1 61.8, 109.4 61.8 Q 115.7 61.8, 119.1 65.6 Q 122.4 69.4, 122.4 76.2 Q 122.4 83.1, 119.0 87.0 Q 115.6 90.9, 109.4 90.9 Q 103.2 90.9, 99.8 87.0 Q 96.4 83.1, 96.4 76.2 M 109.4 87.7 Q 113.7 87.7, 116.1 84.8 Q 118.4 81.9, 118.4 76.2 Q 118.4 70.7, 116.1 67.9 Q 113.7 65.0, 109.4 65.0 Q 105.1 65.0, 102.7 67.8 Q 100.4 70.6, 100.4 76.2 Q 100.4 81.9, 102.7 84.8 Q 105.1 87.7, 109.4 87.7 '' fill=''#000000''/></svg>', '["Butan-1-ol", "Butan-2-ol", "Butanal", "Butane"]', 1, 'NaBH₄ adds a hydride ion to the carbonyl carbon, and the oxygen then gains H⁺. The ketone butanone becomes the secondary alcohol CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₃, butan-2-ol.', '{"0": "The –OH ends up where the C=O was, on carbon 2, not at the end of the chain.", "2": "Reduction does not move the C=O. An aldehyde would need the carbonyl at the end of the chain.", "3": "NaBH₄ reduces C=O to CH–OH. It does not remove the oxygen completely."}', 1),
  ('CHE-2056', 'Chemistry', 'Aldehydes, ketones and carboxylic acids', 'Nucleophilic addition', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'The aldehyde shown reacts with HCN in the presence of a little KCN. What is the product?', '<svg version=''1.1'' xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300px'' height=''180px'' viewBox=''0 0 300 180''><path d=''M 149.3,153.9 L 149.1,77.8'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 149.1,84.4 L 202.3,53.6'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 143.4,74.5 L 196.5,43.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 149.1,77.8 L 96.8,47.7'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;'' /><path d=''M 149.1,81.6 L 149.1,77.8 L 146.5,76.3'' style=''fill:none;stroke:#000000;stroke-width:2.0px;stroke-miterlimit:10;'' /><path d=''M 201.9 39.7 Q 201.9 32.9, 205.3 29.1 Q 208.6 25.3, 214.9 25.3 Q 221.2 25.3, 224.5 29.1 Q 227.9 32.9, 227.9 39.7 Q 227.9 46.6, 224.5 50.5 Q 221.1 54.4, 214.9 54.4 Q 208.7 54.4, 205.3 50.5 Q 201.9 46.7, 201.9 39.7 M 214.9 51.2 Q 219.2 51.2, 221.5 48.3 Q 223.9 45.4, 223.9 39.7 Q 223.9 34.2, 221.5 31.4 Q 219.2 28.5, 214.9 28.5 Q 210.6 28.5, 208.2 31.3 Q 205.9 34.1, 205.9 39.7 Q 205.9 45.5, 208.2 48.3 Q 210.6 51.2, 214.9 51.2 '' fill=''#000000''/><path d=''M 72.1 25.8 L 75.9 25.8 L 75.9 37.8 L 90.4 37.8 L 90.4 25.8 L 94.3 25.8 L 94.3 54.1 L 90.4 54.1 L 90.4 41.0 L 75.9 41.0 L 75.9 54.1 L 72.1 54.1 L 72.1 25.8 '' fill=''#000000''/></svg>', '["CH₃CH₂CN", "CH₃COCN", "CH₃CH(OH)CN", "CH₃CH(CN)₂"]', 2, 'The CN⁻ ion attacks the slightly positive carbonyl carbon (nucleophilic addition). The oxygen then picks up H⁺, giving the cyanohydrin 2-hydroxypropanenitrile, CH₃CH(OH)CN.', '{"0": "The oxygen stays in the product as –OH. It is not removed.", "1": "The C=O does not survive. Its π bond is used to add CN and H.", "3": "Only one HCN adds across the single C=O bond."}', 1)
) AS s
