'' font-family=''Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif'' font-size=''15'' text-anchor=''middle'' dominant-baseline=''middle'' fill=''#000'' font-weight=''bold''>A</text><rect x=''107.0'' y=''122.0'' width=''74'' height=''74'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''118.1'' cy=''170.1'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''169.9'' cy=''170.1'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''118.1'' cy=''147.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''169.9'' cy=''147.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><text x=''144.0'' y=''212.0'' font-family=''Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif'' font-size=''15'' text-anchor=''middle'' dominant-baseline=''middle'' fill=''#000'' font-weight=''bold''>B</text><rect x=''193.0'' y=''122.0'' width=''74'' height=''74'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''218.9'' cy=''184.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''241.1'' cy=''184.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''218.9'' cy=''133.1'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''241.1'' cy=''133.1'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><text x=''230.0'' y=''212.0'' font-family=''Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif'' font-size=''15'' text-anchor=''middle'' dominant-baseline=''middle'' fill=''#000'' font-weight=''bold''>C</text><rect x=''279.0'' y=''122.0'' width=''74'' height=''74'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''304.9'' cy=''184.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''327.1'' cy=''184.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''304.9'' cy=''133.1'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><text x=''316.0'' y=''212.0'' font-family=''Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif'' font-size=''15'' text-anchor=''middle'' dominant-baseline=''middle'' fill=''#000'' font-weight=''bold''>D</text></svg>', '["Figure A", "Figure B", "Figure C", "Figure D"]', 2, 'Unfold one step at a time, starting with the last fold. Each time, every hole is copied as a mirror image across that fold line. The punch goes through 4 layers, so the open sheet has 4 holes.', '{"0": "This shows only the hole in the top layer, but the punch goes through four layers.", "1": "This has four holes, but each is turned a quarter-turn out of place: the holes should be near the top and bottom edges, close to the middle line.", "3": "Each fold doubles the holes, so there must be four, not three."}', 1),
  ('LR-1037', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Paper folding', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'A square sheet of paper is folded as shown (the right half is folded over the left and then the right half is folded over the left), and a hole is punched through it. What does the sheet look like when it is opened out?', '<svg xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''374'' height=''240'' viewBox=''0 0 374 240''><rect x=''50.0'' y=''14.0'' width=''74.0'' height=''74.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><line x1=''87.0'' y1=''10.0'' x2=''87.0'' y2=''92.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''1.4'' stroke-linecap=''round'' stroke-dasharray=''5 4''/><path d=''M 120.0 8.0 Q 87.0 -8.0 56.0 8.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''1.4'' stroke-linejoin=''round'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><polyline points=''63.0,1.0 56.0,8.0 65.0,10.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''1.4'' stroke-linejoin=''round'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><rect x=''150.0'' y=''14.0'' width=''37.0'' height=''74.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><line x1=''168.5'' y1=''10.0'' x2=''168.5'' y2=''92.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''1.4'' stroke-linecap=''round'' stroke-dasharray=''5 4''/><path d=''M 183.0 8.0 Q 168.5 -8.0 156.0 8.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''1.4'' stroke-linejoin=''round'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><polyline points=''163.0,1.0 156.0,8.0 165.0,10.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''1.4'' stroke-linejoin=''round'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><rect x=''250.0'' y=''14.0'' width=''18.5'' height=''74.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''257.4'' cy=''39.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><rect x=''21.0'' y=''122.0'' width=''74'' height=''74'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''28.4'' cy=''147.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''87.6'' cy=''147.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><text x=''58.0'' y=''212.0'' font-family=''Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif'' font-size=''15'' text-anchor=''middle'' dominant-baseline=''middle'' fill=''#000'' font-weight=''bold''>A</text><rect x=''107.0'' y=''122.0'' width=''74'' height=''74'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''114.4'' cy=''147.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''136.6'' cy=''147.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''151.4'' cy=''147.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''173.6'' cy=''147.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><text x=''144.0'' y=''212.0'' font-family=''Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif'' font-size=''15'' text-anchor=''middle'' dominant-baseline=''middle'' fill=''#000'' font-weight=''bold''>B</text><rect x=''193.0'' y=''122.0'' width=''74'' height=''74'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''200.4'' cy=''147.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''222.6'' cy=''147.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><text x=''230.0'' y=''212.0'' font-family=''Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif'' font-size=''15'' text-anchor=''middle'' dominant-baseline=''middle'' fill=''#000'' font-weight=''bold''>C</text><rect x=''279.0'' y=''122.0'' width=''74'' height=''74'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''286.4'' cy=''147.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''345.6'' cy=''147.9'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''286.4'' cy=''170.1'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><circle cx=''345.6'' cy=''170.1'' r=''5.0'' fill=''none'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2''/><text x=''316.0'' y=''212.0'' font-family=''Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif'' font-size=''15'' text-anchor=''middle'' dominant-baseline=''middle'' fill=''#000'' font-weight=''bold''>D</text></svg>', '["Figure A", "Figure B", "Figure C", "Figure D"]', 1, 'Unfold one step at a time, starting with the last fold. Each time, every hole is copied as a mirror image across that fold line. The punch goes through 4 layers, so the open sheet has 4 holes.', '{"0": "This undoes only the first fold. The second fold, at the middle of the left half, also copies the hole.", "2": "This undoes only the second fold and misses the copies on the right half.", "3": "Both folds run from top to bottom, so the holes all stay at the same height. None can appear in the bottom half."}', 1)
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, figure, options, answer, why, wrong, mdcat, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11, s.column12, s.column13,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('LR-1038', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Counting figures', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'How many triangles are there in the figure?', '<svg xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300'' height=''220'' viewBox=''0 0 300 220''><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''48.8'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''251.2'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''48.8'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''251.2'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''116.3'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''183.7'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/></svg>', '["3", "5", "4", "6"]', 3, 'Name the points on the base from left to right. Every triangle has the top vertex plus two of the four base points, and there are 4 × 3 ÷ 2 = 6 ways to choose two.', '{"0": "3 counts only the small triangles side by side.", "1": "5 misses the large outer triangle.", "2": "4 counts the three small ones and the outer one but not the two made of two small ones."}', 1),
  ('LR-1039', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Counting figures', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'How many triangles are there in the figure?', '<svg xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300'' height=''220'' viewBox=''0 0 300 220''><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''48.8'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''251.2'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''48.8'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''251.2'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''99.4'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''150.0'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''200.6'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''99.4'' y1=''110.0'' x2=''200.6'' y2=''110.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/></svg>', '["10", "16", "20", "12"]', 2, 'Lines from the top to the base split the base into 4 parts, giving 5 × 4 ÷ 2 = 10 triangles with the base as their bottom side. The line across makes another 10 with that line as their bottom side. Total 20.', '{"0": "10 counts only the triangles standing on the base.", "1": "16 misses some of the triangles that sit above the cross line.", "3": "12 counts the small triangles and the outer ones but misses most of the combined ones."}', 1),
  ('LR-1040', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Counting figures', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'How many triangles are there in the figure?', '<svg xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300'' height=''220'' viewBox=''0 0 300 220''><line x1=''64.0'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''236.0'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''236.0'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''236.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''236.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''64.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''64.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''64.0'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''64.0'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''236.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''64.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''236.0'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/></svg>', '["4", "8", "6", "10"]', 1, 'The diagonals cut the square into 4 small triangles. Two neighbouring small triangles together make a larger triangle, and there are 4 such pairs. 4 + 4 = 8.', '{"0": "4 counts only the small triangles.", "2": "6 misses two of the larger triangles that use a full diagonal.", "3": "10 counts some triangles twice."}', 1),
  ('LR-1041', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Counting figures', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'How many triangles are there in the figure?', '<svg xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300'' height=''220'' viewBox=''0 0 300 220''><line x1=''64.0'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''236.0'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''236.0'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''236.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''236.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''64.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''64.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''64.0'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''64.0'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''236.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''64.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''236.0'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''150.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''64.0'' y1=''110.0'' x2=''236.0'' y2=''110.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/></svg>', '["16", "8", "12", "20"]', 0, 'The diagonals and the two middle lines make 8 small triangles. There are 4 medium triangles, each with a whole side of the square as its base and the centre as its top. There are 4 large triangles, each half of the square cut off by a diagonal. 8 + 4 + 4 = 16.', '{"1": "8 counts only the smallest triangles.", "2": "12 misses one size of triangle.", "3": "20 counts some shapes that are not triangles."}', 1)
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, figure, options, answer, why, wrong, mdcat, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11, s.column12, s.column13,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('LR-1042', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Counting figures', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'How many triangles are there in the figure?', '<svg xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300'' height=''220'' viewBox=''0 0 300 220''><line x1=''48.8'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''251.2'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''251.2'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''150.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''48.8'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''200.6'' y2=''110.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''200.6'' y1=''110.0'' x2=''99.4'' y2=''110.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''99.4'' y1=''110.0'' x2=''150.0'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/></svg>', '["4", "3", "6", "5"]', 3, 'Joining the midpoints makes 4 small triangles, and the outer triangle is a fifth.', '{"0": "4 forgets the large outer triangle.", "1": "3 counts only the corner triangles and forgets the middle one and the outer one.", "2": "6 counts a shape that isn''t a triangle."}', 1),
  ('LR-1043', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Counting figures', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'How many triangles are there in the figure?', '<svg xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300'' height=''220'' viewBox=''0 0 300 220''><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''94.1'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''94.1'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''240.4'' y2=''89.7'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''240.4'' y1=''89.7'' x2=''59.6'' y2=''89.7'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''59.6'' y1=''89.7'' x2=''205.9'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''205.9'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''150.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/></svg>', '["5", "8", "10", "15"]', 2, 'The five points of the star are 5 small triangles. Each of the 5 straight lines is also the long side of one larger, flat triangle, made of a point of the star and part of the middle. 5 + 5 = 10.', '{"0": "5 counts only the points of the star.", "1": "8 misses some of the larger triangles.", "3": "15 counts some triangles more than once."}', 1),
  ('LR-1044', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Counting figures', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'How many squares are there in the figure?', '<svg xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''220'' height=''220'' viewBox=''0 0 220 220''><line x1=''24.0'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''196.0'' y2=''196.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''24.0'' y1=''138.7'' x2=''196.0'' y2=''138.7'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''24.0'' y1=''81.3'' x2=''196.0'' y2=''81.3'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''24.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''196.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''24.0'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''24.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''81.3'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''81.3'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''138.7'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''138.7'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''196.0'' y1=''196.0'' x2=''196.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/></svg>', '["9", "14", "10", "13"]', 1, 'There are 9 small squares, 4 squares of 2 × 2 and 1 large 3 × 3 square: 9 + 4 + 1 = 14.', '{"0": "9 counts only the smallest squares.", "2": "10 adds only the large outer square.", "3": "13 misses the large outer square."}', 1),
  ('LR-1045', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Counting figures', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'How many squares are there in the figure?', '<svg xmlns=''http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'' width=''300'' height=''170'' viewBox=''0 0 300 170''><line x1=''28.0'' y1=''146.0'' x2=''272.0'' y2=''146.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''28.0'' y1=''85.0'' x2=''272.0'' y2=''85.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''28.0'' y1=''24.0'' x2=''272.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''28.0'' y1=''146.0'' x2=''28.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''89.0'' y1=''146.0'' x2=''89.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''150.0'' y1=''146.0'' x2=''150.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''211.0'' y1=''146.0'' x2=''211.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/><line x1=''272.0'' y1=''146.0'' x2=''272.0'' y2=''24.0'' stroke=''#000'' stroke-width=''2.2'' stroke-linecap=''round''/></svg>', '["11", "8", "10", "12"]', 0, 'There are 8 small squares and 3 squares of 2 × 2 (one for each pair of neighbouring columns): 8 + 3 = 11.', '{"1": "8 counts only the smallest squares.", "2": "10 misses one of the 2 × 2 squares.", "3": "12 counts the whole 4 × 2 figure, which is a rectangle, not a square."}', 1)
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, figure, options, answer, why, wrong, mdcat, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11, s.column12, s.column13,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('LR-1101', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Analogy', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Surgeon is to scalpel as carpenter is to:', '', '["Wood", "Table", "Workshop", "Saw"]', 3, 'A surgeon cuts with a scalpel; a carpenter cuts with a saw. The pair is a worker and the cutting tool they use.', '{"0": "Wood is what the carpenter works on, not the tool.", "1": "A table is something a carpenter makes.", "2": "A workshop is where a carpenter works."}', 1),
  ('LR-1102', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Analogy', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Thermometer is to temperature as barometer is to:', '', '["Rainfall", "Humidity", "Air pressure", "Wind speed"]', 2, 'A thermometer measures temperature and a barometer measures air (atmospheric) pressure.', '{"0": "Rainfall is measured with a rain gauge.", "1": "Humidity is measured with a hygrometer.", "3": "Wind speed is measured with an anemometer."}', 1),
  ('LR-1103', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Analogy', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Cub is to lion as calf is to:', '', '["Sheep", "Cow", "Horse", "Goat"]', 1, 'A cub is a young lion and a calf is a young cow.', '{"0": "A young sheep is a lamb.", "2": "A young horse is a foal.", "3": "A young goat is a kid."}', 1),
  ('LR-1104', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Analogy', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Pen is to write as knife is to:', '', '["Cut", "Sharp", "Kitchen", "Steel"]', 0, 'The pair is an object and what it is used for: a pen is used to write and a knife is used to cut.', '{"1": "Sharp describes a knife; it is not what the knife is used for.", "2": "Kitchen is a place where a knife is kept.", "3": "Steel is what a knife is made of."}', 1)
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, figure, options, answer, why, wrong, mdcat, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11, s.column12, s.column13,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('LR-1105', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Analogy', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Petal is to flower as page is to:', '', '["Paper", "Word", "Library", "Book"]', 3, 'A petal is a part of a flower and a page is a part of a book.', '{"0": "Paper is what a page is made of, not the whole it belongs to.", "1": "A word is a part of a page, the reverse of the pair.", "2": "A library holds books; a page is not directly a part of a library."}', 1),
  ('LR-1106', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Analogy', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Drought is to water as famine is to:', '', '["Rain", "Hunger", "Food", "Crops"]', 2, 'A drought is a severe shortage of water and a famine is a severe shortage of food.', '{"0": "Rain ends a drought; it is not what a famine lacks.", "1": "Hunger is a result of famine, not the thing in short supply.", "3": "Crops can fail in a famine, but the shortage itself is of food."}', 1),
  ('LR-1107', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Analogy', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Cautious is to reckless as frugal is to:', '', '["Thrifty", "Extravagant", "Careful", "Poor"]', 1, 'Reckless is the opposite of cautious, and extravagant (spending freely) is the opposite of frugal (spending little).', '{"0": "Thrifty means much the same as frugal, not the opposite.", "2": "Careful is close to cautious and does not oppose frugal.", "3": "Poor describes having little money, not a way of spending it."}', 1),
  ('LR-1108', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Analogy', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Kidney is to excretion as lungs are to:', '', '["Respiration", "Circulation", "Digestion", "Oxygen"]', 0, 'The kidneys carry out excretion and the lungs carry out gas exchange for respiration.', '{"1": "Circulation is the work of the heart and blood vessels.", "2": "Digestion is the work of the stomach and intestines.", "3": "Oxygen is a gas the lungs take in, not their function."}', 1)
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, figure, options, answer, why, wrong, mdcat, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11, s.column12, s.column13,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('LR-1109', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Analogy', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Find the pair that is related in the same way as AUTHOR : NOVEL.', '', '["Reader : magazine", "Singer : microphone", "Painter : brush", "Composer : symphony"]', 3, 'An author creates a novel, and a composer creates a symphony: a person and the work they create.', '{"0": "A reader uses a magazine but does not create it.", "1": "A microphone is a singer''s tool, not their work.", "2": "A brush is a painter''s tool; the work would be a painting."}', 1),
  ('LR-1110', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Analogy', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Find the pair that is related in the same way as FISH : SCHOOL.', '', '["Bird : nest", "Bee : honey", "Wolf : pack", "Cow : milk"]', 2, 'A group of fish is called a school, and a group of wolves is called a pack.', '{"0": "A nest is a bird''s home, not a group of birds.", "1": "Honey is what bees make.", "3": "Milk is what a cow produces."}', 1),
  ('LR-1111', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Odd one out', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Which word does not belong with the others?', '', '["Iron", "Wood", "Silver", "Copper"]', 1, 'Iron, silver and copper are metals. Wood is not a metal.', '{"0": "Iron is a metal like the others.", "2": "Silver is a metal like the others.", "3": "Copper is a metal like the others."}', 1),
  ('LR-1112', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Odd one out', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Which word does not belong with the others?', '', '["Kilogram", "Metre", "Centimetre", "Kilometre"]', 0, 'Metre, centimetre and kilometre are units of length. The kilogram is a unit of mass.', '{"1": "The metre is a unit of length.", "2": "The centimetre is a unit of length.", "3": "The kilometre is a unit of length."}', 1)
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, figure, options, answer, why, wrong, mdcat, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11, s.column12, s.column13,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('LR-1113', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Odd one out', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Which word does not belong with the others?', '', '["Sparrow", "Crow", "Eagle", "Bat"]', 3, 'The sparrow, crow and eagle are birds. A bat flies too, but it is a mammal.', '{"0": "The sparrow is a bird.", "1": "The crow is a bird.", "2": "The eagle is a bird."}', 1),
  ('LR-1114', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Odd one out', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Which word does not belong with the others?', '', '["Insulin", "Thyroxine", "Femur", "Adrenaline"]', 2, 'Insulin, thyroxine and adrenaline are hormones. The femur is a bone, the thigh bone.', '{"0": "Insulin is a hormone made by the pancreas.", "1": "Thyroxine is a hormone made by the thyroid gland.", "3": "Adrenaline is a hormone made by the adrenal glands."}', 1),
  ('LR-1115', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Odd one out', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Which number does not belong with the others? 17, 23, 29, 33', '', '["17", "33", "23", "29"]', 1, '17, 23 and 29 are prime numbers. 33 is 3 × 11, so it is not prime.', '{"0": "17 is prime.", "2": "23 is prime.", "3": "29 is prime."}', 1),
  ('LR-1116', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Odd one out', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Which word does not belong with the others?', '', '["Brief", "Massive", "Enormous", "Gigantic"]', 0, 'Massive, enormous and gigantic all mean very large. Brief means short in time, so it is the odd one out.', '{"1": "Massive means very large.", "2": "Enormous means very large.", "3": "Gigantic means very large."}', 1)
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO questions
  (id, subject, chapter, topic, difficulty, source, stem, figure, options, answer, why, wrong, mdcat, stage, free, checks, writer_id, created_at, updated_at)
SELECT s.column1, s.column2, s.column3, s.column4, s.column5, s.column6, s.column7, s.column8, s.column9, s.column10, s.column11, s.column12, s.column13,
  'live', 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('LR-1117', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Odd one out', 'hard', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Which letter group does not belong with the others? ACE, BDF, GIK, MNP', '', '["ACE", "BDF", "GIK", "MNP"]', 3, 'In ACE, BDF and GIK each letter skips one letter of the alphabet (A_C_E). In MNP, M and N are next to each other, so the pattern breaks.', '{"0": "ACE skips one letter each time.", "1": "BDF skips one letter each time.", "2": "GIK skips one letter each time."}', 1),
  ('LR-1118', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Dictionary order', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'If these words are arranged in dictionary order, which comes third? Doctor, Docile, Document, Doctorate', '', '["Doctor", "Docile", "Document", "Doctorate"]', 3, 'In dictionary order: Docile, Doctor, Doctorate, Document. ''Docile'' comes first because i comes before t. ''Doctor'' comes before ''Doctorate'' because it is shorter with the same start, and ''Doctorate'' comes before ''Document'' because t comes before u.', '{"0": "Doctor is second.", "1": "Docile is first.", "2": "Document is last."}', 1),
  ('LR-1119', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Dictionary order', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Which word comes first in dictionary order? Plasma, Plastic, Plaster, Planet', '', '["Plasma", "Plastic", "Planet", "Plaster"]', 2, 'All four start with ''Pla''. The fourth letters are n, s, s, s, and n comes before s, so Planet is first.', '{"0": "Plasma comes after Planet because s comes after n.", "1": "Plastic comes after Plasma and Plaster.", "3": "Plaster comes after Planet and Plasma."}', 1),
  ('LR-1120', 'Logical Reasoning', 'Patterns and sequences', 'Jumbled words', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question', 'Rearrange the letters RETNAI to make a word. What does that word name?', '', '["An animal", "A salt", "A part of the eye", "A plant"]', 2, 'RETNAI rearranges to RETINA, the light-sensitive layer at the back of the eye.', '{"0": "No common animal''s name uses exactly these six letters.", "1": "Nitrate is close, but it needs seven letters with two Ts.", "3": "Rattan is close, but it needs two Ts and no E or I."}', 1)
) AS s
JOIN users u ON u.email = 'hozaiphaa@gmail.com';

